1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Amazing images from inside the human body.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:10,000
They're made possible through medical imaging,

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:17,000
a life-saving technology that began with the accidental discovery of the X-ray.

4
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:23,000
Now, medical imaging, the voyage inward on modern marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,280
Here is the baby's hand and the baby's face.

6
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:39,700
Here's the eye and you can see some movement.

7
00:00:39,820 --> 00:00:41,360
The baby's sucking his thumb here.

8
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:46,600
From the womb to old age.

9
00:00:46,601 --> 00:00:49,860
What we're looking at is a human pelvis,

10
00:00:50,120 --> 00:00:53,760
which has a fracture on one side and is normal on the other side.

11
00:00:55,060 --> 00:00:59,340
Medical imaging has allowed doctors to see deep inside the body,

12
00:00:59,341 --> 00:01:02,740
nearly eliminating the need of exploratory surgery.

13
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:06,620
I don't think anybody really understands how life-saving

14
00:01:06,621 --> 00:01:12,940
and how fantastic medicine has become as a result of the imaging devices.

15
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:18,520
It's been an amazing, just an amazing impact on medicine.

16
00:01:20,140 --> 00:01:24,560
The technology that has made medical miracles routine

17
00:01:24,561 --> 00:01:27,060
was discovered by accident.

18
00:01:27,061 --> 00:01:29,420
completely unexpectedly.

19
00:01:35,380 --> 00:01:38,380
The last quarter of the 19th century

20
00:01:38,381 --> 00:01:41,640
saw an explosion of technological achievement.

21
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:46,740
Motion pictures, Alexander Graham Bell's telephone,

22
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:50,960
Thomas Edison's phonograph.

23
00:01:53,780 --> 00:01:56,460
Physicists believe they were on the verge

24
00:01:56,461 --> 00:02:01,480
of understanding everything about the invisible forces that power the universe.

25
00:02:02,860 --> 00:02:07,880
But then a group of scientists started experimenting with electromagnetism.

26
00:02:08,420 --> 00:02:14,760
They correctly suspected that a connection between electrical particles and magnetic fields

27
00:02:14,761 --> 00:02:19,900
resulted in a spectrum of energy waves, including visible light.

28
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:24,900
They wanted to find out exactly what electricity was.

29
00:02:24,901 --> 00:02:32,300
they knew that electromagnetism was the same general phenomenon, but they didn't know all

30
00:02:32,301 --> 00:02:33,290
the little details.

31
00:02:33,291 --> 00:02:36,780
So they were trying to find out the various particles that appeared

32
00:02:36,781 --> 00:02:39,682
when you set up an electric charge.

33
00:02:39,683 --> 00:02:44,740
To accomplish this, scientists utilized fragile, oddly shaped

34
00:02:44,741 --> 00:02:46,883
hand-blown glass tubes.

35
00:02:46,884 --> 00:02:52,940
These are all variations of a tube created by an English chemist,

36
00:02:52,941 --> 00:02:55,250
William Crookes in 1876.

37
00:02:55,251 --> 00:03:01,700
Crookes conceived a method of pumping nearly all the air out of the

38
00:03:01,701 --> 00:03:03,517
tube, creating a vacuum.

39
00:03:03,518 --> 00:03:09,120
When electric current was applied to the metal electrodes at either end of

40
00:03:09,121 --> 00:03:17,000
the tube, cathode rays, later identified as electrons, would cause the tube to glow and the

41
00:03:17,001 --> 00:03:21,240
plates to turn green.

42
00:03:21,241 --> 00:03:25,900
Exploring cathode rays was the passion of a physics professor at Germany's

43
00:03:25,901 --> 00:03:28,280
University of Wurzburg,

44
00:03:28,281 --> 00:03:33,760
Wilhelm Conrad Röntgen.

45
00:03:33,761 --> 00:03:37,840
Röntgen lived above his laboratory with wife Bertha.

46
00:03:37,841 --> 00:03:40,840
On November 8, 1895,

47
00:03:40,841 --> 00:03:42,780
he made a discovery

48
00:03:42,781 --> 00:03:46,320
that would change the world.

49
00:03:46,321 --> 00:03:50,760
Portrayed here in a 1930s promotional film made by General Electric,

50
00:03:51,280 --> 00:03:55,100
the 50-year-old physicist was experimenting with cathode rays

51
00:03:55,101 --> 00:03:57,860
using a variation of a crook's tube.

52
00:03:59,220 --> 00:04:03,680
He noticed a mysterious greenish glow from across the room,

53
00:04:04,640 --> 00:04:07,000
too far away to be a cathode ray.

54
00:04:09,040 --> 00:04:10,280
Röntgen just stopped.

55
00:04:11,160 --> 00:04:16,100
He stopped everything and pursued this exploration of this mysterious phenomenon.

56
00:04:16,320 --> 00:04:23,320
Rankin began working day and night, taking his meals and even sleeping in the lab.

57
00:04:23,321 --> 00:04:30,320
An avid photographer, he began aiming the tubes at photographic plates.

58
00:04:30,321 --> 00:04:37,320
Rankin found that when certain objects like coins or steel weights were placed between the plates and the rays,

59
00:04:37,321 --> 00:04:41,320
they would imprint bizarre shadow images.

60
00:04:41,321 --> 00:04:48,880
Wrenken knew he was producing a new kind of ray and amazingly it was capable of

61
00:04:48,881 --> 00:04:53,080
penetrating opaque objects such as the steel barrel of his hunting rifle.

62
00:04:53,081 --> 00:05:00,220
Finally Wrenken held his hand between the rays and the fluorescent screen.

63
00:05:00,221 --> 00:05:02,421
What he saw was shocking.

64
00:05:02,422 --> 00:05:08,320
Wilhelm Wrenken was staring at the skeletal shadow of his own hand.

65
00:05:08,321 --> 00:05:17,183
It is rumored that when he finally, after a couple of weeks, told Bertha she better come and look, and Bertha was getting a little bit curious or annoyed, but he

66
00:05:17,184 --> 00:05:18,780
hadn't been eating his meals.

67
00:05:18,900 --> 00:05:23,040
He would rush right back to the lab, whatever he did, and he finally said, you're not going to believe this.

68
00:05:24,680 --> 00:05:33,000
Remkin placed Bertha's hand in front of the ray and captured the haunting image of her wedding ring floating upon a bony finger.

69
00:05:33,001 --> 00:05:38,700
And it is said that she said when she saw her hand, I have seen my death.

70
00:05:39,440 --> 00:05:43,520
Because up until this time, no one ever saw bones unless they were bones of dead people.

71
00:05:44,620 --> 00:05:49,020
A month later, Rankin submitted a handwritten account of his discovery,

72
00:05:49,520 --> 00:05:54,860
along with a photo of the X-ray image, to the Physico-Medical Society of Würzburg.

73
00:05:57,520 --> 00:06:01,260
He identified the ray as X for unknown.

74
00:06:01,261 --> 00:06:08,260
Within weeks, both Rehnken and the X-ray were worldwide phenomena.

75
00:06:08,261 --> 00:06:17,260
In 1901, Rehnken's discovery would be awarded with the first Nobel Prize ever granted in physics.

76
00:06:17,261 --> 00:06:24,260
His mysterious X-rays were soon recognized by physicists for what they really were,

77
00:06:24,261 --> 00:06:31,260
really were electromagnetic waves with a length about one one-thousandth that of visible light.

78
00:06:31,261 --> 00:06:36,007
But to the Western world they were much more.

79
00:06:36,008 --> 00:06:40,860
Within months of their discovery, X-rays would

80
00:06:40,861 --> 00:06:44,662
impact healthcare, the legal system and forensics.

81
00:06:44,663 --> 00:06:48,540
They would be seen as everything from a tool of the

82
00:06:48,541 --> 00:06:57,100
occult to a novelty amusement, and they would fire the opening shot of a social and sexual

83
00:06:57,101 --> 00:06:57,940
revolution.

84
00:07:04,600 --> 00:07:09,600
Wilhelm Renken published his first report on the discovery of the X-ray on the same

85
00:07:09,601 --> 00:07:15,620
day the Lumiere brothers screened the first motion picture to a live audience, December

86
00:07:15,621 --> 00:07:17,360
28, 1895.

87
00:07:18,540 --> 00:07:26,620
When Wilhelm Röntgen discovered the X-ray in 1895,

88
00:07:27,320 --> 00:07:30,000
he sought neither fame nor fortune.

89
00:07:30,840 --> 00:07:32,400
Instead of filing for a patent,

90
00:07:32,940 --> 00:07:36,420
he shared his method for creating X-rays with the world.

91
00:07:37,220 --> 00:07:41,640
It was a simple process, and it spread like wildfire.

92
00:07:42,640 --> 00:07:46,140
I think it's fair to say that never before,

93
00:07:46,141 --> 00:07:52,560
and perhaps never since has there been a discovery that went from innovation, so to speak,

94
00:07:52,680 --> 00:07:56,580
to implementation so quickly as with the X-ray.

95
00:07:57,140 --> 00:08:02,800
It was put into clinical use literally within days of its announcement.

96
00:08:03,400 --> 00:08:07,100
And in the space of a year, a thousand separate articles were published,

97
00:08:07,340 --> 00:08:11,360
both in scientific and non-scientific journals, on the use of the X-ray.

98
00:08:11,361 --> 00:08:16,840
on January 19th 1896

99
00:08:16,841 --> 00:08:19,280
less than one month after

100
00:08:19,281 --> 00:08:21,040
Rankin published his discovery

101
00:08:21,041 --> 00:08:23,860
14 year old Eddie McCarthy

102
00:08:23,861 --> 00:08:26,240
took a hard spill while ice skating

103
00:08:26,241 --> 00:08:28,640
on the Connecticut River in Hanover New Hampshire

104
00:08:28,641 --> 00:08:32,240
Eddie's doctor Gilman Frost

105
00:08:32,241 --> 00:08:34,200
suspected a fractured wrist

106
00:08:34,201 --> 00:08:36,560
but had no way of knowing for sure

107
00:08:36,561 --> 00:08:40,120
and at that time a fracture

108
00:08:40,121 --> 00:08:41,380
was a clinical diagnosis.

109
00:08:41,640 --> 00:08:43,480
Nobody had ever really seen a fracture

110
00:08:43,481 --> 00:08:46,460
unless the bones were sticking out through the skin.

111
00:08:47,580 --> 00:08:50,440
Dr. Frost took Eddie to Reed Hall,

112
00:08:51,160 --> 00:08:54,340
then the physics building at nearby Dartmouth College.

113
00:08:57,500 --> 00:09:00,660
There, Frost's brother Edwin, a physics professor,

114
00:09:00,661 --> 00:09:04,880
had been testing the school's large collection of vacuum tubes

115
00:09:04,881 --> 00:09:09,400
in search of one capable of generating the amazing x-rays

116
00:09:09,401 --> 00:09:10,520
described by Renkin.

117
00:09:12,520 --> 00:09:16,000
He made an X-ray impression of different objects,

118
00:09:16,120 --> 00:09:19,240
including a pocket knife, using this tube.

119
00:09:21,220 --> 00:09:24,060
The Frost brothers then X-rayed Eddie's wrist,

120
00:09:24,420 --> 00:09:27,320
using this machine, still on display at Dartmouth.

121
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:32,760
First, the wrist is placed on top of a photographic plate.

122
00:09:34,180 --> 00:09:38,320
Current is generated by a transformer called an ABS coil.

123
00:09:39,400 --> 00:09:43,920
The low voltage source would come in and be amplified by this transformer.

124
00:09:44,580 --> 00:09:47,860
And then the voltage would be applied across this tube.

125
00:09:48,340 --> 00:09:57,800
Now the thing that made this tube very special was this oblique mica plate in the middle of the tube which had a metallic coating on it.

126
00:09:57,801 --> 00:10:01,740
And it was the metallic coating that actually generated the x-rays.

127
00:10:03,940 --> 00:10:08,580
Early x-rays required extremely long exposure times.

128
00:10:09,400 --> 00:10:13,580
Young Eddie had to hold his wrist still for nearly 30 minutes.

129
00:10:14,820 --> 00:10:20,833
Fortunately, his discomfort paid off as the resulting radiograph confirmed Dr.

130
00:10:20,834 --> 00:10:23,840
Frost's diagnosis of a fractured wrist.

131
00:10:25,460 --> 00:10:28,160
It's very hard to see the fracture, but it's there.

132
00:10:29,700 --> 00:10:33,060
Eddie McCarthy's X-ray was significant for two reasons.

133
00:10:33,580 --> 00:10:36,840
It was the first clinical X-ray made in America.

134
00:10:36,841 --> 00:10:45,820
and the photograph of the event is believed to be the first ever taken of a scientific experiment in progress.

135
00:10:47,640 --> 00:10:56,220
The rapid use of the X-ray to peer inside the body was matched only by the speed with which others tried to improve them.

136
00:10:56,221 --> 00:11:03,820
Besides being the era's most celebrated inventor,

137
00:11:04,580 --> 00:11:08,360
Thomas Edison might have been its most ingenious marketer.

138
00:11:09,220 --> 00:11:13,620
He and his team went to work immediately after reading of Rentgen's discovery.

139
00:11:16,960 --> 00:11:21,840
They produced more powerful tubes by using thinner glass,

140
00:11:21,841 --> 00:11:25,680
which allowed more x-rays to pass through at a faster rate.

141
00:11:26,220 --> 00:11:37,500
Their efforts quickly led to the fluoroscope, a handheld pyramid-shaped box with a calcium-tongue-state coated screen on one end and an eyepiece on the other.

142
00:11:39,900 --> 00:11:46,980
When the fluoroscope was held in front of an X-ray beam, one could see real-time images.

143
00:11:46,981 --> 00:11:52,780
Edison dramatically demonstrated the wonder of the fluoroscope

144
00:11:52,781 --> 00:11:59,020
to enormous crowds at New York's National Electrical Exposition in 1896

145
00:11:59,021 --> 00:12:01,980
Edison was a showman

146
00:12:01,981 --> 00:12:05,040
people would have to stand on a long line

147
00:12:05,041 --> 00:12:07,940
and then they would walk into a very dark room

148
00:12:07,941 --> 00:12:11,300
which had these eerie colored lights

149
00:12:11,301 --> 00:12:15,420
very dark in the corners like an Egyptian tomb

150
00:12:15,421 --> 00:12:23,020
Each visitor held the fluoroscope to their eyes and saw their very own bones.

151
00:12:23,780 --> 00:12:30,920
It was an extraordinary experience and there were people who when they would get into the room would suddenly be terrified and they would refuse to go through with it.

152
00:12:31,420 --> 00:12:37,540
The people who were afraid said the same thing that Bertha Runken was supposed to have said is they were afraid of seeing their own death.

153
00:12:37,541 --> 00:12:46,040
by bathing his exhibit space in what was described as Egyptian darkness with blood red lamps

154
00:12:46,041 --> 00:12:54,140
Edison was also giving many in the crowd a first look at his most famous invention the electric light

155
00:12:54,141 --> 00:12:58,900
but the atmosphere he created was part of a marketing ploy

156
00:12:58,901 --> 00:13:03,380
based on the public's growing fascination with the supernatural

157
00:13:03,381 --> 00:13:06,160
Jill, are you here?

158
00:13:10,100 --> 00:13:11,040
Thank you.

159
00:13:12,040 --> 00:13:21,120
The end of the 19th century was a time when spiritualism was a very popular idea in many circles.

160
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:27,960
And the idea that there was a connection between this world, the real world that we can see,

161
00:13:28,180 --> 00:13:32,740
and an invisible world of different dimensions was very popular.

162
00:13:32,741 --> 00:13:43,469
The x-ray came out of nowhere, and it was invisible, and it showed that there was a great deal in the world that was invisible to the naked eye that could be seen or

163
00:13:43,470 --> 00:13:46,200
sensed if only you had the right password.

164
00:13:47,740 --> 00:13:56,840
While some believe the x-ray could open the door to a higher dimension, others feared its power to penetrate a more personal one.

165
00:13:57,400 --> 00:14:01,400
There were lots of advertisements for lead-lined underwear.

166
00:14:02,740 --> 00:14:09,540
So that you wouldn't be, you know, at risk of walking down the street and having someone look through your underwear with an x-ray.

167
00:14:11,040 --> 00:14:18,360
Such potential exposure was alarming to a Western society anchored by Victorian principles.

168
00:14:19,220 --> 00:14:22,840
New technology was already threatening those values.

169
00:14:25,240 --> 00:14:31,680
The affection displayed in this short kinetoscope film made by Thomas Edison was considered scandalous.

170
00:14:31,681 --> 00:14:35,620
but the x-ray provided the ultimate threat.

171
00:14:36,380 --> 00:14:39,720
Proper women wore many, many layers of petticoats

172
00:14:39,721 --> 00:14:42,800
and their skirts went down to the bottom of their shoes

173
00:14:42,801 --> 00:14:45,480
because the ankle was considered a very erotic thing to see.

174
00:14:45,680 --> 00:14:49,320
Suddenly you have a machine that makes clothes irrelevant

175
00:14:49,321 --> 00:14:53,000
because you can look right through the clothes, through your skin

176
00:14:53,001 --> 00:14:54,620
until you got to your bones.

177
00:14:55,080 --> 00:14:56,480
And this was shocking.

178
00:14:56,481 --> 00:15:09,348
If the x-ray was going to remove all these layers of opaque substances and enable you to see right through to the bone, then you were exposed, you were naked to the

179
00:15:09,349 --> 00:15:10,049
bone.

180
00:15:11,320 --> 00:15:17,180
X-rays also diminished the authority doctors enjoyed in diagnosing their patients.

181
00:15:18,200 --> 00:15:25,420
Before the x-ray, most doctors were not usually attacked in court for the way they did their work.

182
00:15:25,421 --> 00:15:26,820
They weren't second-guessed.

183
00:15:27,860 --> 00:15:32,080
With the coming of the x-ray, suddenly you could see if a doctor had made a proper diagnosis or not.

184
00:15:34,300 --> 00:15:35,124
Surgeon W.W.

185
00:15:35,125 --> 00:15:41,580
Grant of Denver was renowned for having performed the first appendectomy in the United States.

186
00:15:42,160 --> 00:15:47,960
But when he misdiagnosed the patient's broken hip as only a contusion, he was sued.

187
00:15:49,300 --> 00:15:53,160
The patient had an x-ray taken that revealed the fracture.

188
00:15:54,160 --> 00:15:58,800
He won the legal battle to have it admitted as evidence, and then won the case.

189
00:15:59,560 --> 00:16:04,400
The onus of malpractice would haunt the medical profession from that day forward.

190
00:16:07,500 --> 00:16:10,760
Already a useful tool of both doctors and lawyers,

191
00:16:10,761 --> 00:16:15,300
the newly discovered X-ray could also be used by coroners.

192
00:16:16,460 --> 00:16:21,660
A horrible tragedy in Paris would reveal its importance in the field of forensics.

193
00:16:23,160 --> 00:16:31,920
On May 4th, 1897, a throng of 6,000 people attended an elite philanthropic bazaar.

194
00:16:33,040 --> 00:16:40,720
The highlight was an exhibition of Thomas Edison's kinescope, the precursor to large screen motion pictures.

195
00:16:40,721 --> 00:16:47,460
when a kinescope wire shorted

196
00:16:47,461 --> 00:16:51,000
flames rapidly spread across the wood floor

197
00:16:51,001 --> 00:16:54,320
to large decorative flats that tragically blocked

198
00:16:54,321 --> 00:16:55,940
many of the hall's exits

199
00:16:55,941 --> 00:17:02,420
more than 200 prominent French citizens perished

200
00:17:02,421 --> 00:17:04,800
burned beyond recognition

201
00:17:04,801 --> 00:17:08,700
yet they could be identified by pre-existing fractures

202
00:17:08,701 --> 00:17:11,620
and other injuries thanks to X-rays.

203
00:17:12,640 --> 00:17:15,680
The ability to identify the dead,

204
00:17:16,280 --> 00:17:18,000
to see broken bones,

205
00:17:18,420 --> 00:17:20,500
or to locate a spray of buckshot

206
00:17:20,501 --> 00:17:24,640
were revolutionary advances for science and medicine.

207
00:17:26,880 --> 00:17:29,060
But looming on the horizon

208
00:17:29,061 --> 00:17:32,300
was the X-ray's tragic dark side.

209
00:17:38,700 --> 00:17:49,040
There has probably never been a medical procedure as widely implemented with virtually no testing than the x-ray.

210
00:17:49,420 --> 00:18:00,240
In addition to providing a view inside the body, the wondrous new rays were even used to remove skin blemishes such as moles, acne, and birthmarks.

211
00:18:00,241 --> 00:18:09,520
in one of the more frightening chapters of x-ray usage some doctors believe that x-rays could help

212
00:18:09,521 --> 00:18:17,200
alleviate america's racial problem it was in 1904 and there were newspaper articles

213
00:18:17,201 --> 00:18:24,360
from all over the country from berkeley california from new york city from philadelphia of different

214
00:18:24,361 --> 00:18:30,822
doctors announcing that they could turn Negroes white by exposing them to x-rays.

215
00:18:30,823 --> 00:18:31,620
This was a

216
00:18:31,621 --> 00:18:37,271
period when the Klan was really growing, when lynchings were epidemic in the South.

217
00:18:37,272 --> 00:18:38,020
And so here

218
00:18:38,021 --> 00:18:41,359
you have people of goodwill saying, oh, we'll solve the problem.

219
00:18:41,360 --> 00:18:42,820
We won't have any more black

220
00:18:42,821 --> 00:18:47,453
people. We'll turn them all white. And that this would be relatively harmless.

221
00:18:47,454 --> 00:18:48,700
I don't think anybody

222
00:18:48,701 --> 00:18:50,840
really thought they were going to kill all the black people.

223
00:18:50,841 --> 00:18:59,920
At least two doctors published reports that they had successfully used x-rays to lighten the skin of African Americans.

224
00:19:00,880 --> 00:19:09,020
Though within a year, more enlightened doctors announced the procedure would surely end in severe burns and a painful death.

225
00:19:09,980 --> 00:19:15,140
Further accounts of such treatment disappeared from all records a short time later.

226
00:19:15,141 --> 00:19:26,400
The bizarre episode was indicative of how little people knew about the X-ray and its deadly side effect, radiation poisoning.

227
00:19:28,180 --> 00:19:39,100
Ironically, during the X-ray's glorious early years, French scientists Pierre and Marie Curie were making great strides in isolating the natural element radium.

228
00:19:39,101 --> 00:19:46,560
Radiation is the word they use to identify the invisible energy waves emitted by radium.

229
00:19:48,060 --> 00:19:54,260
Exposure caused blisters and burns that never healed and in many cases led to cancer.

230
00:19:56,320 --> 00:20:00,820
Marie Curie would eventually succumb to her many years of such exposure,

231
00:20:00,821 --> 00:20:05,360
dying of leukemia in 1934 at age 67.

232
00:20:05,361 --> 00:20:11,600
But her pioneering research of radioactivity would save countless lives.

233
00:20:14,620 --> 00:20:24,460
Unfortunately for many others, the realization that x-rays produced an artificial form of radiation would not be discovered until it was too late.

234
00:20:25,680 --> 00:20:30,040
This film, produced by the United States Army, illustrated the danger.

235
00:20:30,041 --> 00:20:38,640
doctors and some technologists who routinely put their hands in the x-ray beam when diagnoses were

236
00:20:38,641 --> 00:20:43,560
being attempted and did so repeatedly began to notice that they were getting x-ray burns and

237
00:20:43,561 --> 00:20:47,540
some of them developed cancers in the areas that have been exposed to x-rays over a period of

238
00:20:47,541 --> 00:20:55,180
months or years even though evidence was mounting that x-ray exposure caused skin damage the

239
00:20:55,181 --> 00:20:59,500
Tremendous medical benefits instilled a reluctance to examine the problem.

240
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:04,020
Instead, x-ray burns were considered idiosyncratic.

241
00:21:05,360 --> 00:21:08,260
That was a term that was used then a lot,

242
00:21:08,400 --> 00:21:14,080
that they would say some people are just more prone to respond to radiation,

243
00:21:14,260 --> 00:21:16,600
that they might be allergic to radiation.

244
00:21:17,180 --> 00:21:19,320
So maybe my neighbor, who's a radiologist,

245
00:21:19,321 --> 00:21:22,400
dies this horrible death from radiation-induced cancer,

246
00:21:22,480 --> 00:21:23,780
but he was prone to it.

247
00:21:23,781 --> 00:21:28,060
I come from better stock. I'm not going to have that allergy. I'll be all right.

248
00:21:29,100 --> 00:21:39,540
X-ray technicians were soon identified by the black glove they wore over whichever badly scarred hand was used to test the potency of their X-ray beams.

249
00:21:40,860 --> 00:21:46,960
Their agonizing deaths would be remembered by a monument in Germany called the X-ray martyrs.

250
00:21:46,961 --> 00:21:54,080
Perhaps the most famous martyr was Thomas Edison's most trusted assistant, Clarence Daly.

251
00:21:55,500 --> 00:22:00,380
Daly was constantly operating Edison's fluoroscopes and other x-ray machines.

252
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:07,280
Soon he began developing sores around his eyes, his hairline receded,

253
00:22:07,680 --> 00:22:12,960
and his fingers developed such vicious sores they had to be amputated one by one.

254
00:22:12,961 --> 00:22:18,860
He died a horrible death. He was in agony. He'd lost most of both arms.

255
00:22:19,200 --> 00:22:25,120
And it was just so devastating that Edison swore off x-rays then and would have nothing to do with it.

256
00:22:25,940 --> 00:22:34,200
25 years later, only months before his own death, Edison was suffering from severe abdominal pain.

257
00:22:34,880 --> 00:22:38,200
His doctor suspected an ulcer and requested x-rays.

258
00:22:38,201 --> 00:22:41,520
The 84-year-old inventor refused.

259
00:22:42,100 --> 00:22:45,260
They were in his mind simply too dangerous.

260
00:22:47,120 --> 00:22:50,860
However, X-rays would become considerably safer

261
00:22:50,861 --> 00:22:54,780
thanks to a scientist at General Electric, William Coolidge.

262
00:22:55,560 --> 00:22:59,020
Generally speaking, the greater the penetrating power

263
00:22:59,021 --> 00:23:03,040
and the greater the intensity of the X-rays produced,

264
00:23:03,860 --> 00:23:06,920
the shorter the time to make a radiograph.

265
00:23:08,200 --> 00:23:12,820
In 1913, Coolidge devised a new X-ray tube.

266
00:23:13,360 --> 00:23:18,580
The Coolidge tube allowed doctors to more accurately pinpoint the focus of the X-rays

267
00:23:18,581 --> 00:23:21,660
and to control the amount of radiation emitted.

268
00:23:22,780 --> 00:23:26,320
Lead casings around the tubes prevented any spillage.

269
00:23:28,340 --> 00:23:32,220
Further benefits would come with the use of intensifying screens,

270
00:23:32,580 --> 00:23:35,340
which converted X-ray energy into light energy,

271
00:23:35,341 --> 00:23:40,860
reducing a patient's exposure time from half an hour to only seconds.

272
00:23:42,060 --> 00:23:49,120
The bulky lead-lined protective suits worn by some x-ray technicians gave way to lead aprons and goggles.

273
00:23:51,280 --> 00:23:55,460
Now, not only safer, x-rays were becoming more effective.

274
00:23:56,260 --> 00:23:59,680
Contrast agents such as barium were used with fluoroscopes.

275
00:24:00,620 --> 00:24:07,900
When swallowed by patients, the barium would provide views that went beyond the bones to actual organs.

276
00:24:10,200 --> 00:24:15,560
The next great advance in X-ray technology would not occur until the 1960s,

277
00:24:15,561 --> 00:24:20,960
and surprisingly, the beetles would play an important role in its development.

278
00:24:20,961 --> 00:24:31,200
Here we see three babies within this woman's womb.

279
00:24:31,201 --> 00:24:35,520
And then right here is where we can see all three of the babies in three different sacks.

280
00:24:36,600 --> 00:24:44,480
In the 1970s, medical imaging took its place on page one of the family photo album, thanks to ultrasound.

281
00:24:44,481 --> 00:24:54,480
This completely safe form of imaging uses sound waves to provide parents their first glimpse at baby, or babies.

282
00:24:54,481 --> 00:24:59,480
The idea of using sound waves to create an image is not a new one.

283
00:24:59,481 --> 00:25:03,480
Pierre Curie explored the possibility in 1877.

284
00:25:03,481 --> 00:25:12,480
But public demand for such technology was precipitated by the events of a calm ocean night in 1912.

285
00:25:12,481 --> 00:25:22,580
If only the Titanic had a way of using sound waves rather than mere sight to detect icebergs, the infamous disaster might never have occurred.

286
00:25:24,980 --> 00:25:36,665
Because of the Titanic tragedy, interest in Curie's sound wave research was renewed, both in hopes of preventing a future catastrophe and actually locating the sunken

287
00:25:36,666 --> 00:25:37,580
luxury liner.

288
00:25:37,581 --> 00:25:41,000
However, those efforts would have to be postponed.

289
00:25:42,480 --> 00:25:50,480
The outbreak of World War I two years later presented an even more pressing problem.

290
00:25:50,481 --> 00:25:53,380
The need to detect enemy submarines.

291
00:25:55,880 --> 00:26:02,580
Attempts to do so using sound wave technology were made, though they were not completely effective.

292
00:26:03,740 --> 00:26:11,960
German submarines operating off our east coast sank 79 merchant ships in the two months following our declaration of war on Germany, December 11th.

293
00:26:11,961 --> 00:26:20,340
In World War II, the Allies depended on a more sophisticated method called sound navigation and ranging, or sonar.

294
00:26:31,040 --> 00:26:37,660
But it was not until the end of the war that strides could be made toward the ultrasound imaging of today.

295
00:26:38,620 --> 00:26:44,800
When the war was over, there was lots of army surplus, navy surplus stuff around,

296
00:26:45,180 --> 00:26:46,560
which they were practically giving away.

297
00:26:47,340 --> 00:26:51,640
And it turned out that there were lots of doctors who had been drafted.

298
00:26:52,280 --> 00:26:54,800
And there were doctors in the United States, there were doctors in England,

299
00:26:54,880 --> 00:26:56,940
there were doctors in Japan, there were doctors in Germany,

300
00:26:57,500 --> 00:27:05,560
all of whom kind of figured that this equipment could some way be adopted to look inside the body.

301
00:27:05,561 --> 00:27:14,440
One of the most successful attempts was made at the University of Colorado Medical School by radiologist Douglas Howery.

302
00:27:15,720 --> 00:27:21,940
Working out of his own basement, Howery combined sonar with another recent innovation, television.

303
00:27:22,660 --> 00:27:24,840
The result was a somascope.

304
00:27:26,180 --> 00:27:30,600
The somascope relied on a thin crystal quartz to create sound waves.

305
00:27:30,601 --> 00:27:39,660
They could penetrate a tub of water, scan an object and send an echo back to a television tube, producing an image.

306
00:27:41,740 --> 00:27:48,600
Another wartime innovation, the computer, would advance somascope technology to ultrasound.

307
00:27:49,820 --> 00:27:59,940
Now doctors could penetrate organs, study the beating heart, or see inside the womb without using radiation.

308
00:28:00,600 --> 00:28:06,480
The computer would also open the door to the next generation of imaging devices.

309
00:28:12,700 --> 00:28:20,880
In the 1960s, X-ray was the world's primary imaging tool, and the Beatles its favorite rock and roll band.

310
00:28:21,540 --> 00:28:24,380
Both would affect the future of medical imaging.

311
00:28:25,440 --> 00:28:27,260
X-rays had limitations.

312
00:28:27,261 --> 00:28:31,680
The brain was difficult to image because it was blocked by the skull.

313
00:28:32,640 --> 00:28:38,700
The only way to x-ray it was through a painful and dangerous process called encephalography,

314
00:28:39,020 --> 00:28:41,960
in which the brain was injected with air.

315
00:28:43,120 --> 00:28:44,800
Slowly breathe in and out, okay?

316
00:28:44,980 --> 00:28:45,680
Okay.

317
00:28:45,681 --> 00:28:46,381
Good.

318
00:28:47,640 --> 00:28:54,200
The advent of computerized tomography, or the CAT scan, would not only image the brain,

319
00:28:54,201 --> 00:29:00,500
but turned ordinary x-rays into vivid 3D images of bone and soft tissue.

320
00:29:03,020 --> 00:29:06,860
This revolution in medicine may have never come about, however,

321
00:29:07,200 --> 00:29:10,080
without a revolution in the music industry.

322
00:29:10,720 --> 00:29:14,080
In the early 60s, the Beatles were well on their way

323
00:29:14,081 --> 00:29:17,140
to becoming the most successful band in history.

324
00:29:18,360 --> 00:29:20,780
Their recording company, Britain's EMI,

325
00:29:20,781 --> 00:29:25,220
used some of the staggering profits to allow one of their engineers,

326
00:29:25,660 --> 00:29:27,020
a self-described tinkerer,

327
00:29:27,500 --> 00:29:32,540
to develop ideas that would take advantage of new computer technology.

328
00:29:33,740 --> 00:29:36,200
The engineer, Godfrey Hounsfield,

329
00:29:36,340 --> 00:29:42,340
began dreaming of ways for computers to make 3D images of objects inside a box.

330
00:29:42,940 --> 00:29:49,320
Soon he turned the challenge into producing 3D images of a brain inside a skull.

331
00:29:50,780 --> 00:30:01,880
The CAT scanner places the X-ray coil in a rotating donut-shaped carriage, capable of taking cross-sectional images from dozens of angles.

332
00:30:03,300 --> 00:30:08,500
Computers analyze and assemble all the X-rays into a single image.

333
00:30:08,501 --> 00:30:22,220
In 1972, EMI, with virtually no experience in medical electronics, began developing the CAT scan, based on Hounsfield's design.

334
00:30:23,820 --> 00:30:34,287
All of a sudden, we have a vehicle that allows us to image the brain, to see the brain substance, the subarachnoid spaces, the ventricles of the brain, to detect and

335
00:30:34,288 --> 00:30:36,380
localize and characterize tumors.

336
00:30:36,381 --> 00:30:46,360
The density discrimination between computed tomography and ordinary x-rays is many, many hundreds of fold greater.

337
00:30:47,180 --> 00:30:57,220
By 1977, EMI had sold 700 of the units, including one, to George Washington Medical Center in Washington, D.C.

338
00:30:58,080 --> 00:30:59,200
Mr. President!

339
00:30:59,201 --> 00:31:05,190
On March 31st, 1981, 26-year-old John Hinckley Jr.

340
00:31:05,191 --> 00:31:11,780
fired six exploding bullets at President Ronald Reagan.

341
00:31:12,300 --> 00:31:16,214
I didn't know I was shot. In fact, I was still asking, what was that noise?

342
00:31:16,215 --> 00:31:17,780
I thought it was firecrackers.

343
00:31:18,360 --> 00:31:23,580
And it was only then that I felt a paralyzing pain, and I learned that the bullet had hit me.

344
00:31:23,581 --> 00:31:32,400
When I walked in, they were just concluding a meeting in the hospital of all the doctors associated with the hospital.

345
00:31:32,840 --> 00:31:36,880
True, when I saw all those doctors around me, too, I said, I hope they were all Republicans.

346
00:31:37,760 --> 00:31:46,060
Wounded in the chest, Reagan's life was saved by using conventional x-rays to locate the bullet and skilled surgery to remove it.

347
00:31:47,300 --> 00:31:52,080
But far more gravely wounded was his press secretary, James Brady.

348
00:31:52,760 --> 00:31:57,480
Saving Brady from a near-fatal head wound was a dramatic and difficult challenge.

349
00:31:58,560 --> 00:32:04,200
The attending radiologist, David Davis, might have been helpless if not for the CAT scan.

350
00:32:04,980 --> 00:32:09,460
Brady was immediately placed in the CT scanner and scanned.

351
00:32:10,220 --> 00:32:12,800
And in a very short period of time, the scan pictures came out,

352
00:32:12,860 --> 00:32:18,860
and it was obvious that Brady had a very large blood clot in his right frontal lobe.

353
00:32:18,861 --> 00:32:23,320
He had a bullet wound entering his forehead and a blood clot in his right frontal lobe.

354
00:32:24,020 --> 00:32:29,120
And neither the neurosurgeon or I would have given you much hope that Brady would have survived at that very moment.

355
00:32:29,340 --> 00:32:31,580
It was pretty scary because he had a big clot.

356
00:32:32,260 --> 00:32:34,240
But the neurosurgeon said, I'm going to move on it.

357
00:32:34,360 --> 00:32:35,800
And he was raced to the OR.

358
00:32:35,801 --> 00:32:42,680
And because he knew exactly what was going on, the CT scan, it wasn't a case of exploring where the lesion was.

359
00:32:42,760 --> 00:32:43,460
He knew where it was.

360
00:32:44,060 --> 00:32:47,020
The CAT scan helped save Brady's life.

361
00:32:47,660 --> 00:32:52,120
Ironically, it also helped save the life of his assailant.

362
00:32:53,240 --> 00:32:59,400
During his trial, Hinkley's attorneys broke new legal ground by submitting a CAT scan of their client's brain.

363
00:33:00,140 --> 00:33:02,400
They claimed it showed evidence of schizophrenia.

364
00:33:03,640 --> 00:33:07,780
Though CAT scans have later proven incapable of detecting such a disorder,

365
00:33:07,781 --> 00:33:10,620
the presentation swayed the jury.

366
00:33:11,260 --> 00:33:15,460
They found Hinkley legally insane, sparing him the death penalty.

367
00:33:15,461 --> 00:33:22,120
The computer formulas that made the sharp 3D images of the CAT scan possible

368
00:33:22,121 --> 00:33:28,820
opened the door to newer imaging techniques that would provide an even deeper view of human anatomy.

369
00:33:30,300 --> 00:33:35,240
In 1971, the combined efforts of a research physician, Raymond Demadian,

370
00:33:35,241 --> 00:33:41,540
and chemist Paul Lauterberg resulted in medical imaging's next great advance,

371
00:33:42,240 --> 00:33:45,360
magnetic resonance imaging, or MRI.

372
00:33:45,460 --> 00:33:53,360
MRI images are produced from inside a large cylinder similar to a CAT scanner.

373
00:33:54,820 --> 00:34:00,760
Inside the cylinder is a magnet capable of producing a powerful electromagnetic field.

374
00:34:01,660 --> 00:34:05,120
Since the atoms that make up human beings are electrical in nature,

375
00:34:05,820 --> 00:34:08,760
they are disrupted when the magnet is activated.

376
00:34:10,160 --> 00:34:13,020
Radio waves are then pulsated through the body.

377
00:34:13,021 --> 00:34:18,560
They gather information about the atoms as they return to their normal alignment

378
00:34:18,561 --> 00:34:21,220
once the magnet is deactivated.

379
00:34:23,220 --> 00:34:27,060
And then the signal that comes back from that pulsation

380
00:34:27,061 --> 00:34:31,860
is constructed into an image of your organs or your brain or your ventricles

381
00:34:31,861 --> 00:34:33,840
or your knee or whatever it is that we're looking at.

382
00:34:34,060 --> 00:34:35,040
It's as simple as that.

383
00:34:35,420 --> 00:34:38,480
There is no X-ray. You have no radiation from it.

384
00:34:38,840 --> 00:34:40,800
It's harmless as far as anybody knows.

385
00:34:43,020 --> 00:34:51,360
While CAT scans provide excellent images of bone and soft tissue, MRIs can see inside the bones.

386
00:34:51,940 --> 00:34:56,240
They produce images of blood vessels, cartilage, bone marrow, and ligaments,

387
00:34:56,640 --> 00:34:59,900
and are especially useful for detecting brain tumors.

388
00:35:02,480 --> 00:35:09,440
In 1993, it became the primary tool for a monumental effort to map the brain.

389
00:35:09,441 --> 00:35:13,500
Brain mapping is defined in its title.

390
00:35:13,800 --> 00:35:19,620
It is the opportunity and the ways that we can map the human brain in health and disease,

391
00:35:19,920 --> 00:35:24,620
define its anatomy and then understand its functions and how it works.

392
00:35:25,580 --> 00:35:34,020
The University of California at Los Angeles is the lead center of the International Consortium for Brain Mapping.

393
00:35:34,021 --> 00:35:44,060
By studying thousands of MRIs of human brains, the consortium is gathering data that can have profound effects on medicine.

394
00:35:44,900 --> 00:35:53,480
For example, they can witness the brain's response to various medications, or actually see the early stages of various diseases.

395
00:35:54,480 --> 00:36:03,320
We can look at the change in blood flow as a reflection of areas in the brain that are changing their activity, either up, more activity, or down.

396
00:36:03,321 --> 00:36:06,420
and that's what those techniques tell us.

397
00:36:06,560 --> 00:36:09,020
They tell us where in the brain something is changing

398
00:36:09,021 --> 00:36:11,440
when the person is doing a particular task.

399
00:36:11,760 --> 00:36:14,720
One of the really exciting parts about functional imaging of the brain

400
00:36:14,721 --> 00:36:17,280
is that you can have a way of understanding

401
00:36:17,281 --> 00:36:20,120
where in the brain things are happening

402
00:36:20,121 --> 00:36:22,500
and relate to certain behaviors.

403
00:36:22,980 --> 00:36:24,660
We can study learning directly.

404
00:36:24,960 --> 00:36:27,120
We can say, okay, how does the brain learn this?

405
00:36:27,700 --> 00:36:29,720
And when is it most efficient at learning that?

406
00:36:29,721 --> 00:36:33,100
age 3, age 50, age 70,

407
00:36:33,800 --> 00:36:36,500
and we can pass on to the educators

408
00:36:36,501 --> 00:36:41,980
the real answers of how and when the brain learns something.

409
00:36:42,600 --> 00:36:44,560
That may have very important implications.

410
00:36:44,940 --> 00:36:47,220
Obvious ones are how we educate our children.

411
00:36:49,200 --> 00:36:54,160
While MRI scans help us understand the brain through structural imaging,

412
00:36:54,161 --> 00:36:59,700
another computer-dependent imaging device can monitor metabolism in cells.

413
00:36:59,720 --> 00:37:04,080
spotting degenerative diseases such as Alzheimer's.

414
00:37:06,060 --> 00:37:11,320
Dr. Michelle Turpagosian headed a program at Washington University in St. Louis

415
00:37:11,321 --> 00:37:15,300
that resulted in the first PET scanner in 1975.

416
00:37:16,620 --> 00:37:20,200
PET, short for positron emission topography,

417
00:37:20,201 --> 00:37:25,740
creates images by tracing small amounts of radioactive materials

418
00:37:25,741 --> 00:37:28,300
that are injected into the bloodstream.

419
00:37:29,720 --> 00:37:36,520
Inside a PET scanner are large rings of more than 20,000 detectors

420
00:37:36,521 --> 00:37:41,000
that work in concert to image the path of the radioactive substance.

421
00:37:42,280 --> 00:37:46,060
It has penetrating radiation, like the x-rays are inside of you.

422
00:37:46,740 --> 00:37:49,960
And so the x-rays come out and they hit the detectors,

423
00:37:50,220 --> 00:37:52,100
and those detectors record every single event.

424
00:37:53,120 --> 00:37:57,120
And then those events don't really look like a picture.

425
00:37:57,380 --> 00:37:59,380
You have to actually reconstruct it.

426
00:37:59,381 --> 00:38:02,440
and what you'd actually make is a 3D picture of your insides.

427
00:38:03,580 --> 00:38:07,980
Once again, to the untrained eye, the image produced by a PET scan

428
00:38:07,981 --> 00:38:11,540
does not look as clear as a CAT scan or even an X-ray.

429
00:38:13,520 --> 00:38:20,740
Instead, doctors interpret the image by how the radioactive tracer interacts with the body's chemistry.

430
00:38:21,880 --> 00:38:28,640
In doing so, they can actually see evidence of disease not visible through other imaging devices.

431
00:38:29,380 --> 00:38:33,740
everybody has said for years that you cannot diagnose Alzheimer's disease

432
00:38:33,741 --> 00:38:36,480
except by autopsy where you cut out the brain and look at it

433
00:38:36,481 --> 00:38:42,300
well since 1980 we've been able to see Alzheimer's disease very easily with the

434
00:38:42,301 --> 00:38:45,800
PET scan it just turns out that big parts of the brain are essentially missing

435
00:38:45,801 --> 00:38:48,040
because that's what's being destroyed

436
00:38:48,041 --> 00:38:54,860
with such powerful imaging devices at their disposal doctors and scientists

437
00:38:54,861 --> 00:38:57,740
were seeing more of the human body than ever before

438
00:38:57,741 --> 00:39:03,160
And with the advent of the Internet, so would the rest of the world.

439
00:39:07,420 --> 00:39:15,060
The magnetic field created by most MRI scanners is 10,000 times greater than that of the Earth.

440
00:39:19,320 --> 00:39:22,860
These amazing images are one of a kind.

441
00:39:22,861 --> 00:39:29,920
They are from the first three-dimensional computer model of a whole human being, a virtual cadaver.

442
00:39:29,921 --> 00:39:41,525
It's called the Visible Human Project and its purpose is to provide anatomy students with a model that can be sliced in thousands of different angles and put back

443
00:39:41,526 --> 00:39:42,600
together again.

444
00:39:42,601 --> 00:39:50,960
That's important for teaching whole body anatomy as you teach a medical student to interact with whole bodies.

445
00:39:50,961 --> 00:39:56,800
You want them to see what a body looks like and be able to visualize what things deep to the surface of the body are.

446
00:39:57,320 --> 00:40:01,020
They only see patients generally from the skin or even from the clothing.

447
00:40:01,440 --> 00:40:08,280
And to visualize where the upper left quadrant of your liver is relative to your abdomen, how deep it is, where it is anterior to posterior,

448
00:40:08,780 --> 00:40:12,560
it's important to get that three-dimensional view across to people.

449
00:40:12,980 --> 00:40:16,700
And being able to reconstruct this in the computer was the driving force here.

450
00:40:16,701 --> 00:40:23,700
The visible human is a marriage of CAT scan, MRI and digital photography.

451
00:40:23,701 --> 00:40:34,700
It was constructed at the University of Colorado and is freely available on the Internet to anyone wishing to digitally dissect it.

452
00:40:38,700 --> 00:40:45,700
One of the more bizarre challenges to creating the visible human was finding its real life model.

453
00:40:46,700 --> 00:40:51,200
A young male cadaver in as good a physical condition as possible.

454
00:40:52,000 --> 00:40:54,640
The ideal choice would be a drug overdose victim,

455
00:40:55,060 --> 00:41:01,140
somebody with no visible signs of trauma and with organs not suitable for donation.

456
00:41:02,340 --> 00:41:06,600
39-year-old Paul Jernigan of Waco, Texas, fit the bill.

457
00:41:07,540 --> 00:41:12,520
When he killed a 75-year-old man in an attempt to steal a microwave oven,

458
00:41:13,060 --> 00:41:16,040
Jernigan was sentenced to die by lethal injection.

459
00:41:16,700 --> 00:41:21,040
The execution was carried out on August 5th, 1993.

460
00:41:22,660 --> 00:41:29,160
Having willed his body to science, Jernigan was air shipped to Colorado soon after his death.

461
00:41:31,480 --> 00:41:36,020
CAT scans and MRI scans were immediately taken by Spitzer's team.

462
00:41:37,720 --> 00:41:44,420
Next, the body was encased in a special gel and frozen beneath cakes of dry ice.

463
00:41:46,700 --> 00:41:48,880
Then came the hard part.

464
00:41:53,220 --> 00:41:58,400
The rock-solid frozen cadaver would have to be cut into quarters

465
00:41:58,401 --> 00:42:04,500
so that it could fit into a custom-made slicing device called a chiro-macrotome.

466
00:42:06,580 --> 00:42:11,440
Then each quarter is then encased or made into a large ice cube.

467
00:42:11,920 --> 00:42:16,180
It poured into a mold, a gelatin, which surrounds that part of the body.

468
00:42:16,700 --> 00:42:21,460
and make a 20 inch tall by less than 22 inch wide by less than 14 inch deep

469
00:42:21,461 --> 00:42:25,540
ice cube if you will that contains the specimen

470
00:42:25,541 --> 00:42:30,480
the cadaver's cube is then placed into the chiral macrotome

471
00:42:30,481 --> 00:42:36,360
one millimeter at a time is sliced off the block

472
00:42:36,361 --> 00:42:39,720
here we've frozen everything solid as a rock the skull

473
00:42:39,721 --> 00:42:43,660
and the brain are both the same kind of hardness so when we cut they react the

474
00:42:43,661 --> 00:42:44,380
same way

475
00:42:44,381 --> 00:42:46,860
and you don't have different kinds of distortion of the brain and the skull.

476
00:42:48,100 --> 00:42:53,240
After each slice is removed, the cube is sprayed with alcohol to melt the frost.

477
00:42:54,100 --> 00:42:56,620
Next, a digital photograph is taken.

478
00:42:57,680 --> 00:43:01,800
More than 1,800 slices and photos are made in all.

479
00:43:04,200 --> 00:43:08,660
The images are then stacked and programmed into an imaging computer,

480
00:43:08,661 --> 00:43:13,400
where virtual slices in any direction can be made by users.

481
00:43:14,380 --> 00:43:20,680
The visible human can also be explored entirely through MRI or CAT scan images.

482
00:43:22,340 --> 00:43:28,560
The project's directors see even greater possibilities for future virtual humans.

483
00:43:30,200 --> 00:43:33,580
Right now it's a good simulator for a cadaver. It acts like a cadaver.

484
00:43:34,240 --> 00:43:37,360
And then we'll keep moving until it acts more like a live human.

485
00:43:38,160 --> 00:43:41,220
A man that has a beating heart, a man that has blood flowing through his body.

486
00:43:41,221 --> 00:43:43,980
we're not there today and it's not happening today

487
00:43:43,981 --> 00:43:44,860
but that will happen

488
00:43:44,861 --> 00:43:48,680
such a future may be close at hand

489
00:43:48,681 --> 00:43:50,360
thanks to rapid advances

490
00:43:50,361 --> 00:43:51,820
in digital technology

491
00:43:51,821 --> 00:43:53,960
and an unquenchable thirst

492
00:43:53,961 --> 00:43:56,480
to witness the magnificent inner workings

493
00:43:56,481 --> 00:43:58,020
of the human body

494
00:43:58,021 --> 00:44:02,300
Wilhelm Rehnken could never

495
00:44:02,301 --> 00:44:03,700
have ever envisioned

496
00:44:03,701 --> 00:44:06,420
that his dogged exploration of a mysterious

497
00:44:06,421 --> 00:44:08,520
glow would lay a foundation

498
00:44:08,521 --> 00:44:09,900
for later scientists

499
00:44:09,901 --> 00:44:13,900
who would invent a wide array of powerful imaging techniques.

500
00:44:14,900 --> 00:44:18,940
Their genius will surely inspire the future of medical imaging,

501
00:44:19,280 --> 00:44:25,100
one that holds promise of seeing deeper, further, and with exquisite clarity.

